.Dd October 25, 2026
.Dt MKCACERTS 8
.Os
.Sh NAME
.Nm mkcacerts
.Nd "generate a Java PKCS#12 truststore from the trusted certificate store"
.Sh SYNOPSIS
.Nm
.Op Fl Fnv
.Op Fl D Ar destdir
.Op Fl d Ar distbase
.Op Fl L Ar localbase
.Nm
.Fl -version
.Sh DESCRIPTION
The
.Nm
utility generates a Java-compatible PKCS#12 truststore from the
trusted certificates maintained by
.Xr certctl 8 ,
so that a Java Virtual Machine's default truststore
.Pq Ev javax.net.ssl.trustStore
can be derived from the system truststore without invoking a JVM or
.Ic keytool
to build it.
.Pp
For the truststore
.Nm
produces to actually become a JVM's default truststore, the
vendor-bundled
.Pa lib/security/cacerts
of the target
.Pa java/openjdk Ns Ar N
port must not shadow it.
Those ports support an
.Va OPENJDK_SYMLINK_CACERTS
build variable which, when set, removes the bundled
.Pa cacerts
file and replaces it with a symbolic link to the given path instead;
setting it to the path
.Nm
writes to requires rebuilding the desired
.Pa java/openjdk Ns Ar N
port from source with that variable defined, e.g. in
.Pa /etc/make.conf .
.Pp
.Nm
reads every certificate in
.Ev TRUSTDESTDIR
and writes a PKCS#12 file to
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${LOCALBASE}/etc/ssl/cacerts
containing the same certificates, each with its
.Xr certctl 8
hashed file name
.Pq Pa <hash>.<N>
reused verbatim as its PKCS#12
.Ic friendlyName .
No new subject hash is computed and no certificate is excluded,
renamed, or re-validated;
.Nm
trusts
.Xr certctl 8
to have already performed that work.
.Pp
The following options are available:
.Bl -tag -width 4n
.It Fl D Ar destdir
Specify the DESTDIR (overriding values from the environment).
.It Fl d Ar distbase
Specify the DISTBASE (overriding values from the environment).
.It Fl F
Derive each certificate's PKCS#12
.Ic friendlyName
from its subject, appending the
.Xr certctl 8
.Pa <hash>.<N>
file name in parentheses
.Pq e.g. Dq Li "Example CA (deadbeef.0)"
to guarantee uniqueness and let the name always be traced back to
its file; falls back to
.Pa <hash>.<N>
alone where no name can be derived from the subject.
This is purely cosmetic and has no effect on the truststore's
trust decisions.
.It Fl L Ar localbase
Specify the LOCALBASE (overriding values from the environment).
.It Fl n
Dry-run mode.
Do not write the truststore, only report what would have been
written.
Note that, like
.Xr certctl 8 Ns 's
own
.Fl n ,
the destination directory tree is still created even in dry-run mode.
.It Fl v
Verbose mode.
Print detailed information about each certificate found and each
action taken.
.It Fl -version
Print the
.Nm
version number and exit.
.El
.Pp
Each certificate found in
.Ev TRUSTDESTDIR
is read following symbolic links;
a dangling symbolic link or an unparseable file is skipped with a
warning rather than aborting the run.
If
.Ev TRUSTDESTDIR
does not exist or contains no usable certificate,
.Nm
exits with an error and writes nothing, since a truststore
Java loads successfully but which contains zero trust anchors would
fail far from the actual root cause.
.Pp
.Nm
otherwise behaves like
.Xr certctl 8
itself: certificates are written in the same order, and the output
file is written atomically and with the same permissions.
.Sh ENVIRONMENT
.Bl -tag -width "TRUSTDESTDIR, CERTDESTDIR"
.It Ev DESTDIR
Absolute path to an alternate destination directory to operate on
instead of the file system root, e.g.
.Dq Li /tmp/install .
.It Ev DISTBASE
Additional path component to include when locating
.Ev TRUSTDESTDIR .
This must start with a slash, e.g.
.Dq Li /base .
.It Ev LOCALBASE
Location for local programs.
Defaults to the value of the user.localbase sysctl which is usually
.Pa /usr/local .
.It Ev TRUSTDESTDIR , Ev CERTDESTDIR
Directory to read trusted certificates from, overriding
.Ev DESTDIR
and
.Ev DISTBASE
entirely.
Default:
.Pa ${DESTDIR}${DISTBASE}/etc/ssl/certs ,
the same directory
.Xr certctl 8
writes to.
.El
.Sh EXIT STATUS
.Ex -std
.Sh SEE ALSO
.Xr openssl 1 ,
.Xr certctl 8
.Sh HISTORY
.Nm
first appeared as a standalone utility in 2026, as an interim
substitute for
.Xr certctl 8
itself producing a Java-compatible truststore directly.
.Sh AUTHORS
.An -nosplit
.Nm
was written by
.An Michael Osipov Aq Mt michaelo@FreeBSD.org .
